{"reason": "The execution results show that the bi-encoder's validation log loss (0.3642) has significant headroom and its ensemble weight is only 0.1, indicating limited discriminative power. The initial plan's proposed changes (larger projection, gradual unfreezing, Manifold Mixup, Stochastic Depth) aim to increase capacity and reduce overfitting. Historical successful experiments used similar capacity increases (larger projection, gradual unfreezing) to improve bi-encoder performance, while avoiding pitfalls like full unfreezing or excessive dropout. The training curve shows a sharp drop then plateau, suggesting overfitting mitigation via Stochastic Depth and Mixup is appropriate. I select 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' as the modules to modify because the model architecture needs capacity improvement and the training strategy needs better regularization.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Increase the projection MLP from 256-dim to 512-dim with a 2-layer bottleneck (1024->1024->512). Remove aggressive early layer freezing (freezing first 12 layers) and implement a differential learning rate schedule: layers 0-12 get 0.1x base LR, layers 12-18 get 0.5x, layers 18-24 + custom heads get 1.0x. Reduce dropout in StyleMoE from 0.2 to 0.1, and add Stochastic Depth (DropPath) with rate 0.1 to the last 6 transformer layers (18-23) by replacing residual connections with stochastic depth during training. These changes are inspired by successful historical experiments that used larger projection and gradual unfreezing to improve bi-encoder discriminative power, while the regularization adjustments avoid overfitting pitfalls seen in failed experiments with fixed high dropout. Preserve all existing model variable names and forward signatures.", "training_evaluation": "Add Manifold Mixup augmentation applied to token embeddings before StyleAttentionPooling with alpha=0.3, implemented only during training by interpolating input embeddings and labels. Integrate this into the training loop without changing validation/testing paths. Additionally, adjust the learning rate schedule to accommodate the differential rates: keep linear warmup (10%) + linear decay, but ensure the optimizer parameter groups reflect the new layer-wise LR assignment. These modifications directly address the overfitting pattern (train-val gap) observed in the execution results, and align with historical successful experiments that used similar augmentation to improve generalization. Maintain all existing evaluation functions and submission generation logic unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show that the bi-encoder's validation log loss (0.3642) has significant headroom and its ensemble weight is only 0.1, indicating limited discriminative power. The initial plan's proposed changes (larger projection, gradual unfreezing, Manifold Mixup, Stochastic Depth) aim to increase capacity and reduce overfitting. Historical successful experiments used similar capacity increases (larger projection, gradual unfreezing) to improve bi-encoder performance, while avoiding pitfalls like full unfreezing or excessive dropout. The training curve shows a sharp drop then plateau, suggesting overfitting mitigation via Stochastic Depth and Mixup is appropriate. I select 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' as the modules to modify because the model architecture needs capacity improvement and the training strategy needs better regularization.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Increase the projection MLP from 256-dim to 512-dim with a 2-layer bottleneck (1024->1024->512). Remove aggressive early layer freezing (freezing first 12 layers) and implement a differential learning rate schedule: layers 0-12 get 0.1x base LR, layers 12-18 get 0.5x, layers 18-24 + custom heads get 1.0x. Reduce dropout in StyleMoE from 0.2 to 0.1, and add Stochastic Depth (DropPath) with rate 0.1 to the last 6 transformer layers (18-23) by replacing residual connections with stochastic depth during training. These changes are inspired by successful historical experiments that used larger projection and gradual unfreezing to improve bi-encoder discriminative power, while the regularization adjustments avoid overfitting pitfalls seen in failed experiments with fixed high dropout. Preserve all existing model variable names and forward signatures.",
    "training_evaluation": "Add Manifold Mixup augmentation applied to token embeddings before StyleAttentionPooling with alpha=0.3, implemented only during training by interpolating input embeddings and labels. Integrate this into the training loop without changing validation/testing paths. Additionally, adjust the learning rate schedule to accommodate the differential rates: keep linear warmup (10%) + linear decay, but ensure the optimizer parameter groups reflect the new layer-wise LR assignment. These modifications directly address the overfitting pattern (train-val gap) observed in the execution results, and align with historical successful experiments that used similar augmentation to improve generalization. Maintain all existing evaluation functions and submission generation logic unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows overfitting (best at epoch 4, then rapid degradation) and the complex bi-encoder with StyleMoE and contrastive loss introduces excessive parameters for the dataset size (~15k). Hand-crafted features contribute minimally (LR weight 0.15). Historical successful experiments (e.g., pure AutoModelForSequenceClassification fine-tuning) achieved logloss ~0.3-0.5, while our ensemble plateaued at 0.307. The failed experiment attempted complex ensembles with feature engineering noise. To follow the initial plan's direction of simplifying representation learning, we replace the custom bi-encoder with a standard AutModelForSequenceClassification (DeBERTa-v3-large), remove hand-crafted features, and use cosine annealing with SWA instead of linear scheduler+early stopping. This aligns with successful historical approaches and avoids the overfitting pattern observed.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all hand-crafted feature extraction functions (stylometric, readability, POS, n-grams, punctuation sequences) and associated scaling/variance thresholding. Only keep text loading, stratified split, and tokenization. For XGBoost, use only the bi-encoder embeddings (which will come from the new simpler model) and a simple TF-IDF of word unigrams+bigrams (max_features=5000). This eliminates noisy low-quality features that contributed minimally to the ensemble, forcing XGBoost to rely on high-quality embeddings and lexical patterns. The historical successful experiment with pure transformer fine-tuning showed that feature engineering is unnecessary and harmful.", "model_design": "Replace the entire SpookyAuthorBiEncoder (StyleAttentionPooling, StyleMoE, projection layers) with a simple AutoModelForSequenceClassification from HuggingFace using 'microsoft/deberta-v3-large', with dropout=0.1 in the classification head. Remove all custom loss functions (ContrastiveAuthorLoss) and manifold mixup; use standard cross-entropy with label smoothing (0.1). This aligns with the initial plan's goal of reducing complexity and overfitting. Historical successful experiments show this architecture achieves logloss ~0.3-0.5 without complex components.", "training_evaluation": "Switch to a simpler training loop: use AdamW with a single learning rate (5e-6) for all parameters (remove differential LRs), cosine annealing scheduler (T_max=20), and Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) over the last 10 epochs after the best epoch. Remove gradient clipping and warmup. Set batch_size=16, epochs=25, early_stopping_patience=5 on validation logloss. The initial plan identified cosine annealing+SWA as beneficial to find flatter minima, and historical experiments confirm that a single LR with cosine decay works well."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows overfitting (best at epoch 4, then rapid degradation) and the complex bi-encoder with StyleMoE and contrastive loss introduces excessive parameters for the dataset size (~15k). Hand-crafted features contribute minimally (LR weight 0.15). Historical successful experiments (e.g., pure AutoModelForSequenceClassification fine-tuning) achieved logloss ~0.3-0.5, while our ensemble plateaued at 0.307. The failed experiment attempted complex ensembles with feature engineering noise. To follow the initial plan's direction of simplifying representation learning, we replace the custom bi-encoder with a standard AutModelForSequenceClassification (DeBERTa-v3-large), remove hand-crafted features, and use cosine annealing with SWA instead of linear scheduler+early stopping. This aligns with successful historical approaches and avoids the overfitting pattern observed.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all hand-crafted feature extraction functions (stylometric, readability, POS, n-grams, punctuation sequences) and associated scaling/variance thresholding. Only keep text loading, stratified split, and tokenization. For XGBoost, use only the bi-encoder embeddings (which will come from the new simpler model) and a simple TF-IDF of word unigrams+bigrams (max_features=5000). This eliminates noisy low-quality features that contributed minimally to the ensemble, forcing XGBoost to rely on high-quality embeddings and lexical patterns. The historical successful experiment with pure transformer fine-tuning showed that feature engineering is unnecessary and harmful.",
    "model_design": "Replace the entire SpookyAuthorBiEncoder (StyleAttentionPooling, StyleMoE, projection layers) with a simple AutoModelForSequenceClassification from HuggingFace using 'microsoft/deberta-v3-large', with dropout=0.1 in the classification head. Remove all custom loss functions (ContrastiveAuthorLoss) and manifold mixup; use standard cross-entropy with label smoothing (0.1). This aligns with the initial plan's goal of reducing complexity and overfitting. Historical successful experiments show this architecture achieves logloss ~0.3-0.5 without complex components.",
    "training_evaluation": "Switch to a simpler training loop: use AdamW with a single learning rate (5e-6) for all parameters (remove differential LRs), cosine annealing scheduler (T_max=20), and Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) over the last 10 epochs after the best epoch. Remove gradient clipping and warmup. Set batch_size=16, epochs=25, early_stopping_patience=5 on validation logloss. The initial plan identified cosine annealing+SWA as beneficial to find flatter minima, and historical experiments confirm that a single LR with cosine decay works well."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan identifies overfitting with a sharp U-shaped validation curve and an underweighted transformer in the ensemble (weight 0.1). Similar historical experiments show that SWA and LLRD effectively improve generalization for fine-tuned transformers (successful experiments with 'I will merge the three code snippets...' used standard fine-tuning with cosine annealing and no custom features). The current code uses hand-crafted features that drown out transformer signal, as seen in failed experiments ('I'll merge the data processing...') where feature engineering noise hurt performance. The most promising changes are: (1) replace hand-crafted features with a simple TF-IDF-only pipeline to reduce noise, (2) incorporate SWA into training to average weights from later epochs, (3) use LLRD to protect pretrained layers, and (4) apply class-balanced loss to handle imbalance. These target the root cause of poor transformer fine-tuning while leveraging historical successes.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all hand-crafted stylometric, readability, and POS features. Keep only TF-IDF n-gram features (character and word) and punctuation sequence features, which are already effective for XGBoost and Logistic Regression as shown in the initial plan. Simplify to: extract char n-grams (2-4, 3-5, 4-6) with max_features=2000 each, word n-grams (1-3) with max_features=4000, and punctuation n-grams (2-4) with CountVectorizer. Concatenate all sparse features into a single matrix. This reduces noise and aligns with successful historical experiments that used pure TF-IDF without hand-crafted features.", "model_design": "Replace the current AutoModelForSequenceClassification with a custom model that includes Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) support in its training loop. The model architecture remains the same: DeBERTa-v3-large classifier head with dropout 0.1. Add an SWA callback that, starting from epoch 3 (after warmup), maintains an averaged copy of model weights after each epoch. The final model used for inference is the averaged weights. Additionally, implement layer-wise learning rate decay (LLRD) by setting the classifier head's learning rate to 2e-5 and backbone layers to 2e-6. Use AdamW optimizer. This directly addresses overfitting and underfitting seen in the initial plan.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear warmup scheduler with cosine annealing schedule (T_max=40) with warmup restarts for 10% of steps. Train for 40 epochs with early stopping patience of 5 on SWA validation loss (compute loss using averaged model). Use class-balanced (CB) loss with beta=0.999 to re-weight samples inversely to effective number per class. Modify Compute_log_loss to handle row normalization as in evaluation. Save the SWA model checkpoint. This matches historical successful experiments that used cosine annealing and class weights to improve generalization."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identifies overfitting with a sharp U-shaped validation curve and an underweighted transformer in the ensemble (weight 0.1). Similar historical experiments show that SWA and LLRD effectively improve generalization for fine-tuned transformers (successful experiments with 'I will merge the three code snippets...' used standard fine-tuning with cosine annealing and no custom features). The current code uses hand-crafted features that drown out transformer signal, as seen in failed experiments ('I'll merge the data processing...') where feature engineering noise hurt performance. The most promising changes are: (1) replace hand-crafted features with a simple TF-IDF-only pipeline to reduce noise, (2) incorporate SWA into training to average weights from later epochs, (3) use LLRD to protect pretrained layers, and (4) apply class-balanced loss to handle imbalance. These target the root cause of poor transformer fine-tuning while leveraging historical successes.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all hand-crafted stylometric, readability, and POS features. Keep only TF-IDF n-gram features (character and word) and punctuation sequence features, which are already effective for XGBoost and Logistic Regression as shown in the initial plan. Simplify to: extract char n-grams (2-4, 3-5, 4-6) with max_features=2000 each, word n-grams (1-3) with max_features=4000, and punctuation n-grams (2-4) with CountVectorizer. Concatenate all sparse features into a single matrix. This reduces noise and aligns with successful historical experiments that used pure TF-IDF without hand-crafted features.",
    "model_design": "Replace the current AutoModelForSequenceClassification with a custom model that includes Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) support in its training loop. The model architecture remains the same: DeBERTa-v3-large classifier head with dropout 0.1. Add an SWA callback that, starting from epoch 3 (after warmup), maintains an averaged copy of model weights after each epoch. The final model used for inference is the averaged weights. Additionally, implement layer-wise learning rate decay (LLRD) by setting the classifier head's learning rate to 2e-5 and backbone layers to 2e-6. Use AdamW optimizer. This directly addresses overfitting and underfitting seen in the initial plan.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear warmup scheduler with cosine annealing schedule (T_max=40) with warmup restarts for 10% of steps. Train for 40 epochs with early stopping patience of 5 on SWA validation loss (compute loss using averaged model). Use class-balanced (CB) loss with beta=0.999 to re-weight samples inversely to effective number per class. Modify Compute_log_loss to handle row normalization as in evaluation. Save the SWA model checkpoint. This matches historical successful experiments that used cosine annealing and class weights to improve generalization."
  }
}"}

